<Base
  title="Roadmap — Tuile"
  description="Tuile's milestones, in implementation order, with the acceptance criteria each one has to pass before the next begins."
  kicker="Roadmap"
  heading="Milestones, in order, with the bar each one has to clear."
  lede="A milestone is finished when every one of its criteria passes — not when the code exists. The order is deliberate: later milestones must never require breaking a seam an earlier one established."
>
  <p class="note">
    <strong>The north star</strong> is a rendered globe: streamed terrain draped with raster
    imagery, produced end to end in Rust. The 3D Tiles engine is the foundation that gets
    there; the globe is the load that proves it at planetary scale.
  </p>

  <h2>MG — the globe</h2>

  <div class="milestone">
    <h3>Quantized-mesh terrain and draped imagery <span class="tag">structuring target</span></h3>
    <p>
      Terrain decoding — header, delta-encoded vertex arrays, high-water-mark indices, edge
      skirts, octahedral normals — producing the same decoded content type a glTF tile
      produces, so the renderer needs no change. A geographic quadtree with per-level
      availability and per-level geometric error. A tile-source abstraction that makes the
      traversal, the cache and the geometry server generic over where tiles come from.
      Raster imagery providers, projections, per-tile texture coordinates, and the single
      point where geometry and imagery meet.
    </p>
    <p class="crit">
      <strong>Done means:</strong> a real terrain tile decodes into a coherent mesh; a globe
      patch renders without jitter at any scale, which is the proof that the f64 rebasing is
      real; the same patch draped with imagery displays textured, with provider attributions
      shown; the traversal refines terrain by screen-space error with no terrain-specific
      selection code; and a build with no provider credentials at all still works against a
      free source.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>M1 — core and reference renderer</h2>

  <div class="milestone">
    <h3>The MVP that proves everything else is possible</h3>
    <p>
      Full parsing of the tile tree in both versions of the standard, external tilesets
      included. Content decoding for both container generations. A pure, tested traversal:
      frustum culling, replace-with-hold-until-ready, additive refinement, request
      prioritisation. The protocol module — the message types, the two content forms, the
      <code>GeometryStream</code> trait, and its in-process and HTTP-pull bindings. Fetcher
      implementations for the filesystem and for HTTP. The wgpu backend with a minimal
      physically-based pipeline and relative-to-centre rendering. A windowed viewer with an
      orbit camera and live statistics.
    </p>
    <p class="crit">
      <strong>Done means:</strong> a green workspace test run with traversal coverage on a
      hand-written mini-tileset; a green WebAssembly check on the core; both content
      generations displayed correctly with no jitter at maximum zoom; no frame over 33 ms
      attributable to decoding, which forces decode off the render thread; and zero
      <code>unwrap</code> or <code>panic</code> on the core's production paths.
    </p>
    <p class="crit">
      The viewer consumes the geometry server <em>exclusively</em> through the trait. That
      constraint is the whole point of the milestone: it is what guarantees that switching
      to a remote transport later touches no renderer code.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>M2 — network middleware</h2>

  <div class="milestone">
    <h3>Socket streaming, static mode, and a cache you can remove <span class="tag planned">not yet in the workspace</span></h3>
    <p>
      The network binding of the streaming protocol: one socket session per viewer, one
      geometry server per session, a message envelope versioned from its first byte, and
      geometry serialised as glb. A native serving binary pointed at a directory or at
      object storage. The cache trait with a no-op, a memory-bounded and a disk
      implementation, selected by configuration — a cache that can be switched off entirely
      when something upstream already caches. Correct HTTP semantics for the static mode:
      entity tags, immutable cache control on content, range requests, configurable
      cross-origin policy. An edge-worker build of the same service, sharing the routes
      rather than forking them.
    </p>
    <p class="crit">
      <strong>Done means:</strong> the remote viewer matches the local one not just visually
      but in its <em>selection sequence</em> for a replayed camera path — a programmatic
      test, not an eyeball; independent third-party 3D Tiles web viewers consume a served
      tileset without error; end-to-end session tests cover cancellation, timeouts and
      malformed messages; the bounded cache respects its budget and the no-op cache is a
      true passthrough.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>M2.5 — raster imagery in the core</h2>

  <div class="milestone">
    <h3>Because untextured geometry needs something draped over it</h3>
    <p>
      An imagery-provider trait and its implementations, web-mercator and geographic
      projections, the quadtree mapping that picks imagery tiles at a density matching the
      geometry's screen-space error, the extra texture coordinates on decoded content, and
      the multiply in the render backend.
    </p>
    <p class="crit">
      <strong>Done means:</strong> an untextured tileset plus an imagery layer displays
      draped, attributions are shown, and the imagery memory budget is respected.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>M3 — USD and USDZ export</h2>

  <div class="milestone">
    <h3>A geographic area as a portable scene</h3>
    <p>
      A bounding box and a target error in, a USDZ out: decoded content converted to USD
      text and packaged into a conformant archive — every entry stored uncompressed, every
      payload aligned to 64 bytes, root layer first. Time-sampled animation from a
      timestamped polyline, so a recorded trajectory replays inside the scene. Pure Rust, no
      C++ dependency, and <strong>no change whatsoever to the server</strong>: the exporter
      is an ordinary tileset consumer.
    </p>
    <p class="crit">
      <strong>Done means:</strong> exported archives open in the standard USD viewer and in
      mobile augmented-reality viewers without a blocking warning; an animated export plays
      on its own; the export is deterministic, same inputs to same bytes; and it is bounded,
      with a clear error rather than an unbounded download.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>M4 and beyond</h2>

  <p>Kept possible, deliberately not implemented early:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>The scene-index integration.</strong> A generative procedural inside an
      offline renderer's scene graph, consuming the streaming protocol — the designated
      second consumer, and the target that justifies how clean the trait had to be. A
      C ABI crate on the Rust side, a plugin on the C++ side, and nothing leaking between
      them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A facade for platform-native rendering</strong> on phones, tablets and
      headsets — which is what forces multi-view traversal, because a stereo pair has to
      share one selection or the eyes disagree at refinement boundaries.
    </li>
    <li><strong>JavaScript bindings</strong> of the core, for web renderers.</li>
    <li><strong>A facade for a real-time game engine.</strong></li>
    <li>
      <strong>A control plane</strong> for authentication, indexing and streamed
      invalidations — never a replacement for the HTTP data plane.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Tile generation on the fly</strong> from columnar geospatial sources.</li>
    <li><strong>Point clouds and gaussian splats.</strong></li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    There is no generic foreign-function facade and there will not be one. Each host gets a
    facade shaped like that host's own idioms, because a lowest-common-denominator C API
    would be worse for every one of them.
  </p>

  <h2>Where things actually stand</h2>

  <p class="note">
    This page describes the plan, not a claim of completion. What the workspace contains
    today is the honest signal: the core, the terrain and planetary crates, the wgpu backend
    and its viewers, the native and browser transports, the offline bake-and-replay pipeline
    and the USD side are all present; <strong>no server crate is</strong>. Read
    <code>docs/03-roadmap.md</code> in the repository for the per-criterion state, and the
    <a href="/docs">API reference</a> for what each crate currently exposes.
  </p>

  <p class="next">Next → <a href="/getting-started">Getting started</a></p>
